    "answer": {
      "short": "Use the lowest common denominator $(n+1)!$; multiply only the second fraction by $\\dfrac{n+1}{n+1}$ to obtain $\\dfrac{n+2}{(n+1)!}$.",
      "worked": "The lowest common denominator is $(n+1)!$ because $(n+1)!=(n+1)n!$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{(n+1)!}+\\frac{1}{n!}&=\\frac{1}{(n+1)!}+\\frac{n+1}{(n+1)!}\\\\\\frac{1}{(n+1)!}+\\frac{1}{n!}&=\\frac{n+2}{(n+1)!}\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
